DX Film Edge Barcode — format provenance and usability
==========================================================================

This file records where the DX film edge barcode symbology comes from,
what governs it, and the basis on which this project implements and
redistributes it.

It is an ENGINEERING INVENTORY, NOT LEGAL ADVICE. Items marked [TO VERIFY]
have not been independently confirmed by the author.


ORIGIN
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

Part of Kodak's "DX" (Digital indeX) encoding system for 35mm film,
introduced in 1983. The specific two-track latent-image barcode printed
along the film edge below the sprocket holes (distinct from the
Interleaved 2 of 5 barcode on the film cartridge, and from the
camera-readable DX CAS checkerboard) was patented by Eastman Kodak:
US Patent 4,965,628A, "Photographic Film With Latent Image Multi-Field
Bar Code and Eye-Readable Symbols" (Olliver, Johnson, Patton; filed 1989,
granted 23 October 1990). The broader DX encoding system was later folded
into ANSI PH1.14 / NAPM IT1.14, and is now part of ISO 1007 (a paywalled
standard; not consulted or relied on here).


SPECIFICATION
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

The exact field structure implemented here was cross-verified across
three independent sources:
  - US Patent 4,965,628A itself, read directly from its own page images
    (not automated text extraction). Its high-level summary states a
    7-bit frame-number field; this conflicts with every other source and
    with real film samples.
  - The raw wikitext of Wikipedia's "DX encoding" article, itself citing
    a 2017 peer-reviewed archival-science paper (Gschwind, Zbinden,
    Trumpy & Delaney, "Color negatives at the demise of silver halides,"
    ICOM-CC 18th Triennial Conference) that empirically analyzed real
    developed film samples: 6 start bits, 7 bits product code, 1
    unassigned bit, 4 bits generation, 7-bit frame/half-frame number, 1
    unassigned bit, 1 parity bit, 4 stop bits.
  - Zint (libzint, BSD-3-Clause)'s `dxfilmedge.c`, used only as a
    black-box behavioural reference (never copied), confirms a 6-bit
    frame number (not 7) and clarifies the two "unassigned" bit positions
    are fixed separator bits (always 0): one immediately after the 7-bit
    product code, one immediately after the half-frame flag. This is the
    structure this project implements, since it is the one consistent
    with a real, independently-verified open-source implementation and
    real film samples, not merely a patent's own high-level abstract.

Specification TEXT (the patent, the Wikipedia article, the cited academic
paper) is copyrighted by its authors/publishers. The SYMBOLOGY it
describes — a defunct-for-new-manufacture 1980s film marking scheme — is
not a work of authorship. This project implements the symbology from its
own original code; it does not reproduce, redistribute or excerpt any of
the cited texts or Zint's source.


PATENT STATUS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

US 4,965,628A was filed in 1989 and granted 23 October 1990; under the
pre-1995 17-years-from-grant US patent term, it expired around 2007.
[TO VERIFY] A related extension patent (frame-number printing,
EP 0838718B1 / US 5,872,616A) was filed in the mid-1990s and expired
around 2017 under the 20-years-from-filing term used for patents filed
after 8 June 1995. [TO VERIFY] No evidence of any live patent covering
this symbology was found.


TRADEMARK
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

"DX" and "Kodak" are understood to be trademarks of Eastman Kodak
Company. [TO VERIFY] No trademark search was carried out. A trademark
does not restrict implementing a symbology; it restricts branding — how
a product names and presents itself. This project's `dxfilmedge` format
id and documentation use the name descriptively, to identify the
historical film-marking scheme being implemented, not as a product brand
or a claim of Kodak endorsement.


IMPLEMENTATION BASIS IN THIS PROJECT
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

The bit-field layout (start pattern, product-code field, separator,
generation field, optional frame-number/half-frame fields, parity bit,
stop pattern), the fixed clock-track synchronization patterns, and the
parity algorithm (XOR of all data-track bits between the start and stop
patterns) are original Sythos data and code, verified as described above
against the patent's own drawings and cross-checked against real-sample
literature and one independent open-source implementation used
exclusively as a black-box behavioural reference. No source code, table
or image asset from any other barcode implementation is copied or
shipped.


CONCLUSION
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

On the basis of the references below, the DX film edge barcode is
understood to be freely implementable and redistributable: its
originating and extension patents are both expired, and its structure is
documented across multiple independent, cross-referenced public sources.
This project's implementation of it is distributed under the MIT License
with no additional restriction.

References relied upon:
